## Methods Applied So Far

- Data inspection and structural assessment.
- Missing-value and duplicate checks.
- Descriptive statistical analysis.
- Comparison of means, medians, and standard deviations.
- Distribution analysis using histograms.
- Coefficient of variation (CV) to compare relative variability across product categories.
- Interquartile range (IQR) method to identify potential outliers.
- Comparison of original and filtered statistics to investigate the influence of potential outliers.

## Preliminary Findings

The initial exploratory data analysis has produced the following observations:

- **Data quality:** The dataset contains 440 observations and 8 columns. Initial checks found no missing values or duplicate rows.
- **Spending distributions:** Initial descriptive statistics and histogram analysis suggest that spending distributions are positively skewed, with some customers spending substantially more than others.
- **Variability across categories:** The coefficient of variation (CV) indicates that relative spending variability differs across product categories. Delicassen has the highest CV among the six categories examined, while Fresh has the lowest.
- **Potential outliers:** The Interquartile Range (IQR) method identified 28 potential upper-end outliers in Milk and 27 in Delicassen.
- **Influence of potential outliers:** Excluding the 27 potential upper-end outliers from Delicassen reduced its mean spending from approximately 1,524.87 to 1,092.06 and its median from 965.50 to 838.00.

These findings are preliminary. Further analysis will investigate spending differences across customer channels and geographical regions, explore the influence of unusual observations, and develop a more complete understanding of customer spending patterns.


